#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
gen_future_opcodes.py - generate the "future-expansion opcode inventory".

Every opcode/form that is valid in 32-bit (and 16-bit) protected/real mode
and is NOT implemented by dismount is written as a commented-out entry:

  * legacy one-byte, two-byte (0F) and x87 forms are written inline in
    Source/dismount/proc/ia32/opcodeTable.cpp, as a block between the markers
        // BEGIN FUTURE-OPCODES (generated by tests/ia32/tools/gen_future_opcodes.py)
        // END FUTURE-OPCODES
    placed just before the "// End marker" of each table.  Where the operands
    are expressible with the existing ia32dis::OperandType enum, the
    ready-to-uncomment table entry follows the SDM-style line.
  * 0F38 / 0F3A three-byte maps and all VEX / EVEX forms are written to
    tests/ia32/opcodeTableFuture.inc (comment-only, not built).

Sources (read-only):
  * opcodeTable.cpp           what dismount implements (parsed, not compiled)
  * nasm insns.dat            candidate forms with CPU-level / feature flags
  * Intel SDM Vol. 2 text     authority for opcode bytes and 64-bit-only rules
  * Intel SDM Appendix A text opcode maps, used for the mnemonic cross-check

Usage:
  gen_future_opcodes.py [--ref-dir DIR] [--check] [--write]
    --ref-dir DIR  directory with sdm-vol2.txt and sdm-appendixA-opcode-map.txt
                   (default: $DISMOUNT_SDM_REF). Optional: the generation runs
                   from the repository alone (insns.dat is in tests/ia32/tools,
                   and the SDM answers it needs are in FROZEN_SDM_ANSWERS).
    --check        needs the SDM files: print the SDM cross-check report; exit 1
                   if any SDM opcode has no insns.dat form and is not in
                   SUPPLEMENT. Exit 2 when the SDM files are not found.
    --write        rewrite the two outputs (default: report only)

The script is deterministic and re-runnable: the inline blocks are replaced,
and the .inc file is rewritten from scratch.
"""

